<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="tetrahydrofolate biosynthesis II" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260303222105">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-3742" dataSource="PlantCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;b&gt;General information&lt;/b&gt;: Tetrahydrofolate (vitamin B9) and its derivatives, commonly termed folates, are vital cofactors for enzymes mediating one-carbon-transfer reactions |CITS: [9190084]| |CITS: [10785666]|. Folates are involved in a wide range of key metabolic functions including the biosynthesis of methionine, purines, thymidylate and panthotenate. In plants folates are also involved in photorespiration, amino acid metabolism and chloroplastic protein biosynthesis |CITS: [11960743]| |CITS: [12644692]|. The fluxes through C1 pathways mediated by folates are especially high for methylated compounds such as the secondary metabolites lignin, alkaloids and betaines and for primary metabolites such as choline, pectine and chlorophyll |CITS: [11337394]|. Although the biosynthetic steps in microbial folate biosynthesis essentially mirror the plant variant several differences are noteworthy. In contrast to the bacteria pathway (|FRAME:FOLSYN-PWY|) which predominantly takes place in the cytosol, the plant variant of tetrahydrofolate biosynthesis is split between three subcellular compartments |CITS: [11960743]|. Several plant enzymes involved in the tetrahydrofolate biosynthesis differ from their microbial counterparts with regard to functionality, regulation and composition |CITS: [9190084]| |CITS: [14745019]| |CITS: [15500462]|. Plants prefer the polyglutamylated forms of folates (compare |FRAME:PWY-2161|, |FRAME: PWY-2161B-PMN|) since the turnover rate of those compounds is markedly increased |CITS: [9190084]| |CITS: [SCOTT00]| |CITS: [KIRK94]|and meets the high demands for folates as observed in plants |CITS: [11960743]|. In addition the conjugated forms of folate facilitate the retention of the vitamin within the cell and its subcellular compartments |CITS: [1916088]|. Bacteria, fungi, and plants synthesize folates &lt;i&gt;de novo&lt;/i&gt;, but humans are unable to synthesize folate and need a dietary supply. Lack of folate is the most serious vitamin deficiency decease for humans and results in reduced DNA synthesis and cell division rate. Folates are, to a varying degree, all unstable, due to the oxidative cleavage into pteridine and pPABA glutamate maoities which is additionally promoted by light. The high demand for folate in fundamental metabolic processes and their chemical susceptibility to oxygen and light may be part of the difficulties to prevent folate deficiency even in highly developed countries and has aroused intensive research to genetically engineer the folate biosynthetic pathway |CITS:[SCOTT00]|. &lt;b&gt;Special information&lt;/b&gt;: Folates are tripartite molecules and are made up of pterin, p-aminobenzoate (pABA) and glutamate moieties. In plants the pterin moiety is formed from GTP in the cytosol, which couples to pABA (synthesized in plastids) in mitochondria followed by subsequent glutamylation and reduction steps which may take place in cytosol, mitochondria and plastids |CITS: [11960743]| |CITS: [11752472]|. The one-carbon units are either attached to the N-5 of the pterin moiety, to the N-10 of the pAPA moiety or are brigded in between those two (e.g. 5,10-methenyl or methylene-THF) |CITS: [BASSET05]|. Increased efforts in research to unravel the plant folate biosynthesis have recently resulted in the cloning of the missing plant enzymes - aminodeoxychorismate synthase (ADCS) |CITS: [14745019]|, aminodeoxychorismate lyase (ADC lyase) |CITS: [15500462]| and dihydroneopterin aldolase (DHN aldolase) |CITS: [15107504]| |CITS: [15165863]|completing the enzymatic steps in folate biosynthesis. In the pABA branch a reaction unique to plants has been identified, which reversibly converts pABA to its glucose ester by a cytosolic glucosyltransferase. It has been shown that most of the pAPA in plants exists as glucose ester. Although the physiological function of this reaction remains to be elucidated the glucose ester was proposed to be involved in storage and transportation processes of pAPA |CITS: [12668665]|. The complete set of folate enzymes is only present in mitochondria. However, the recent discovery that folylpolyglutamate synthases are present in cytosol, mitochondria and plastids with each of them encoded by a different gene in &lt;i&gt;Arabidopsis thaliana&lt;/i&gt; |CITS: [11752472]| points to the fact that at least parts of the pathway can be carried out independently in those compartments. Interestingly, the enzyme hydrolyzing the polyglutamylated folates (&amp;gamma;-glutamyl hydrolase) has been found to be an extracellular enzyme in plants |CITS: [8912628]|. Consequently, the transport and exact conversion of folates and their derivatives within the different cell compartments and their regulation pattern remains to be clarified before successfully attempting the endeavor to genetically engineer this pathway.</Description>
